WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.797
Picture cooking your best dish for a friend's party.

2
00:00:05.797 --> 00:00:08.239
At home, it comes out perfect every time.

3
00:00:08.467 --> 00:00:14.511
At your friend's house, the oven runs hotter, there is no fresh garlic, and the salt is a different kind.

4
00:00:14.733 --> 00:00:18.985
The dish fails, and you stand in their kitchen saying: but it works at home.

5
00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:21.552
Software has the same problem.

6
00:00:21.552 --> 00:00:29.236
Your test passes on your Mac with Python 3.14, and fails on the CI machine, which has Python 3.12.

7
00:00:29.236 --> 00:00:31.118
Nobody touched the code.

8
00:00:31.118 --> 00:00:33.392
Everything around it changed.

9
00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:37.980
Wouldn't it be nice to carry the kitchen along with the dish?

10
00:00:37.980 --> 00:00:42.029
Pack it like a lunchbox: the meal plus its own fork, salt and napkin.

11
00:00:42.233 --> 00:00:48.491
Into the box goes the app, the runtime that runs its code, the libraries it uses, and its settings.

12
00:00:48.700 --> 00:00:49.849
Then you seal it.

13
00:00:49.849 --> 00:00:53.227
That sealed, read-only package is called an image.

14
00:00:53.227 --> 00:00:56.741
Docker is the tool that builds images and runs them.

15
00:00:56.967 --> 00:01:02.719
And the same image runs the same way on your Mac, on a CI machine, and on a cloud server.

16
00:01:02.719 --> 00:01:04.916
The kitchen travels with the dish.

17
00:01:06.733 --> 00:01:08.679
Now open one box and run it.

18
00:01:08.679 --> 00:01:10.625
So what is actually running?

19
00:01:10.625 --> 00:01:13.196
A tiny computer inside your computer?

20
00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:20.451
That is a fair guess, because there is an older way that works exactly like that: a virtual machine, or VM.

21
00:01:20.451 --> 00:01:22.757
It pretends to be a whole computer.

22
00:01:22.967 --> 00:01:26.322
It boots a full operating system with its own kernel.

23
00:01:26.322 --> 00:01:33.348
The kernel is the core of an operating system: it starts programs, hands out memory, and talks to the hardware.

24
00:01:33.567 --> 00:01:35.836
Booting all of that takes many seconds.

25
00:01:35.836 --> 00:01:39.212
If a container were that, it would be a busy little place.

26
00:01:39.433 --> 00:01:40.478
Let's test it.

27
00:01:40.478 --> 00:01:42.643
Alpine is a tiny Linux image.

28
00:01:42.643 --> 00:01:45.853
We ask it to list every process it can see.

29
00:01:46.067 --> 00:01:48.910
A real computer runs hundreds of programs.

30
00:01:48.910 --> 00:01:51.212
How many will this container list?

31
00:01:51.212 --> 00:01:52.295
Pause and guess.

32
00:01:54.500 --> 00:01:54.825
One.

33
00:01:54.825 --> 00:01:57.504
Just ps itself, running as PID 1.

34
00:01:57.504 --> 00:02:04.163
A PID is the number Linux gives each running program, and 1 goes to the first one.

35
00:02:04.367 --> 00:02:06.669
Well, that is not a busy computer.

36
00:02:06.669 --> 00:02:08.497
That is one lonely program.

37
00:02:09.200 --> 00:02:11.010
Here is what really happens.

38
00:02:11.010 --> 00:02:14.112
Down at the bottom is the kernel of the machine.

39
00:02:14.112 --> 00:02:17.796
Above it run lots of ordinary programs, called processes.

40
00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:20.384
A container is one more of those.

41
00:02:20.384 --> 00:02:22.046
A normal Linux process.

42
00:02:22.046 --> 00:02:24.719
No second computer, no second kernel.

43
00:02:24.933 --> 00:02:27.692
But the kernel gives it two extra things.

44
00:02:27.692 --> 00:02:28.567
First, walls.

45
00:02:28.567 --> 00:02:34.018
Like a lunchbox in the office fridge: the food inside cannot see the other boxes.

46
00:02:34.018 --> 00:02:36.373
Linux calls these walls namespaces.

47
00:02:36.600 --> 00:02:43.933
Inside its walls, the process gets its own list of programs, its own network, its own name, and its own files.

48
00:02:43.933 --> 00:02:46.000
That is why ps saw only itself.

49
00:02:46.233 --> 00:02:47.410
Second, a budget.

50
00:02:47.410 --> 00:02:51.215
A cap on how much processor time and memory it may use.

51
00:02:51.215 --> 00:02:54.744
Linux calls this a cgroup, short for control group.

52
00:02:54.967 --> 00:02:58.067
Go past the memory cap, and the kernel kills it.

53
00:02:58.900 --> 00:03:02.558
So a container is a process, with walls and a budget.

54
00:03:02.558 --> 00:03:03.938
Not a tiny computer.

55
00:03:03.938 --> 00:03:08.285
A process that was told, very firmly, to mind its own business.

56
00:03:08.900 --> 00:03:11.907
Start two more containers, and here is the twist.

57
00:03:11.907 --> 00:03:17.063
They all use the same kernel, like lunches that all go through one office microwave.

58
00:03:17.267 --> 00:03:18.622
Nothing has to boot.

59
00:03:18.622 --> 00:03:21.333
So a container starts in about a second.

60
00:03:21.933 --> 00:03:23.771
Now a puzzle for Mac users.

61
00:03:23.771 --> 00:03:26.903
A Mac has its own kernel, and it is not Linux.

62
00:03:26.903 --> 00:03:29.489
So which kernel runs these containers?

63
00:03:29.489 --> 00:03:30.579
Pause and guess.

64
00:03:32.800 --> 00:03:34.982
The Linux kernel of one small VM.

65
00:03:34.982 --> 00:03:43.245
Docker Desktop, the Mac app that installs Docker, quietly starts it in the background, and every container shares its kernel.

66
00:03:44.567 --> 00:03:46.339
Back to the lunchbox shop.

67
00:03:46.339 --> 00:03:51.791
One sealed image can start many containers, and each one is its own opened copy.

68
00:03:52.000 --> 00:03:58.040
When the app writes a file, it lands in a thin writable layer that belongs to that copy only.

69
00:03:58.040 --> 00:04:00.313
Like ketchup you add after opening.

70
00:04:00.313 --> 00:04:02.196
The other boxes never see it.

71
00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:05.752
Remove the container, and its ketchup goes with it.

72
00:04:05.752 --> 00:04:07.789
The image itself never changes.

73
00:04:08.700 --> 00:04:10.278
Speed has a price, though.

74
00:04:10.278 --> 00:04:15.072
Because every container shares one kernel, a kernel bug can affect all of them.

75
00:04:15.072 --> 00:04:19.623
A container can only run programs built for that kernel and that processor.

76
00:04:19.623 --> 00:04:22.657
And whatever it writes is lost when you remove it.

77
00:04:23.367 --> 00:04:25.154
So the moral of the story.

78
00:04:25.154 --> 00:04:27.078
The image is the sealed box.

79
00:04:27.078 --> 00:04:31.958
A container is an opened copy: just a process, with walls and a budget.

80
00:04:31.958 --> 00:04:35.532
And they all share one kernel, the office microwave.

81
00:04:36.133 --> 00:04:36.906
Quick check.

82
00:04:36.906 --> 00:04:41.477
You start ten containers from one image, and one of them writes a file.

83
00:04:41.477 --> 00:04:43.087
Do the other nine see it?

84
00:04:45.300 --> 00:04:45.511
No.

85
00:04:45.511 --> 00:04:49.372
It sits in that one container's own layer, its ketchup.

86
00:04:49.372 --> 00:04:52.531
The image, and the other boxes, never change.
